bbapprove help tui — the interactive TUI (-i).

Keys: work in rounds: mark rows (x, ctrl+a for all eligible), then act. a m M d c act on the marked rows, or
on the cursor row when nothing is marked; acted rows are un-marked.
  a  approve only (any row but IGNORED, SKIP included)
  m  approve and merge, eligible rows only
  M  approve and merge anyway (overrides SKIP); always asks y/N and names what it overrides
  d  decline; asks for the comment, which is required
  c  comment
  r  refresh: re-read those same rows (one pr view each); cursor and marks stay
  R  reload the list: rows are updated in place, new PRs are added at the bottom, and a PR that left the list is
     re-read and, when merged or declined elsewhere, stays as a dim MERGED or DECLINED row that cannot be marked
A refresh or reload keeps a row's FAIL, NOT MERGED or APPROVED until the PR itself changed (build, gate, reviews,
comments, tasks, title).
On the cursor row a and m run at once; on marked rows they ask y/N first. A failed approval never stops the
merge: the merge is tried anyway and the approval error shows as a warning on the row. ? lists every key.
The TUI stays open after every action; only q / ctrl+c quits. --no-mouse or BBAPPROVE_MOUSE=0 turns mouse capture off.

Columns: STATUS (see help rules), then
  🔨 build        ✓ passed, ✗ failed, … running, ? unknown or none
  🔎 SonarQube    ✓ quality gate passed, ✗ failed, ? unknown
  👍 approval     ✓ approved (by someone), ✗ needs work (a reviewer requested changes), – no reviews yet
  💬              comment count (tasks excluded)
  ✅              open tasks
  PROJECT/repo#id the PR reference, then the title

Look: the cursor row has a subtle full-width background and the › marker; marked rows show a bold yellow ▌ bar;
the mouse-hovered row a fainter background. With NO_COLOR only the markers remain.

Status bar: two lines that never wrap. Line 1 is the transient message or active prompt, otherwise the cursor
row's PR URL (where o goes) with the version dim at the right; on narrow terminals the URL is shortened in the
middle (https://host/…/pull-requests/4961) so the PR number and the "o open" hint stay visible. Line 2 is
"N PRs, M marked" and as many key hints as fit.

Hints follow the Shift key: by default line 2 shows:
  a approve  m merge  d decline  c comment  i ignore  x mark  o open  ? help  q quit
While you HOLD Shift it switches to the Shift set, and releasing Shift restores the default:
  M force merge (skips the rules)  ctrl+a mark all eligible  x mark  o open  ? help  q quit
Neither set wraps: trailing items are dropped to fit, and "o open" is the last one dropped before "? help" and
"q quit". The ? screen always lists both sets. This needs a terminal that speaks the kitty keyboard protocol
(Ghostty, kitty, recent iTerm2, WezTerm, foot, Alacritty): bbapprove requests the enhancements (key release
events, all keys as escape codes with associated text and alternate keys) and detects support from the
terminal's reply. In any other terminal (Terminal.app, tmux without passthrough, ...) the hints stay on the
default set and every key works the same. The Shift state is also cleared when the window loses focus or a key
arrives without Shift, in case a release is lost. BBAPPROVE_KITTY=0 stops bbapprove requesting the
enhancements (the hints never change; Bubble Tea still asks for basic key disambiguation).

Exit summary: on q / ctrl+c the TUI prints what was done this session to the scrollback: a table in the CLI
format with only the actions taken (MERGED, APPROVED, NOT MERGED, DECLINED, COMMENTED, FAIL, IGNORED / UN-IGNORED), then a
tally such as "3 merged, 1 declined, 1 failed" (zero counts omitted), or "No changes made." Actions still
running are listed as WORKING… and the tally ends "(still running)": quitting does not wait for them. One line
per PR and kind of action. Cut to the terminal width on a TTY, never truncated when piped.

Mouse: on by default: click a row to move the cursor, click the first three columns to toggle its mark,
wheel scrolls 3 rows, hover highlights. Click the PR URL in the footer (or the "o open" hint) to open it, the same as
pressing o. Clicks elsewhere outside the rows are ignored, and the mouse is ignored while a text prompt is open.
Capturing the mouse disables the terminal's native text selection and cmd-click on links; hold Shift to bypass it
in most terminals (Ghostty, iTerm2, kitty).
